`,t(e.p,{children:"None of this was hard to copy. A few years ago, reverse engineering a page like this meant hours in devtools. Now I can point an AI agent at a live site and get back its CSS, its timings, and a working version on my own stack in a single sitting."}),`
`,t(e.p,{children:"That changes what's scarce. Taking a design apart is cheap now. Coming up with it in the first place isn't, and the hard part was never the CSS. It's what the designers chose to leave out. Nothing on that page moves unless it's telling you something. The tree opens because you entered a section. The bar fills because you read further. The title shows up because the real one just left. There's no parallax, no hover flourish, no hero image. They borrowed the look of a terminal and stopped well short of making the page pretend to be one. Each of those is a judgment about when to stop, and you can't get that from a stylesheet. I could measure every animation to the millisecond and still not know which ones they tried and threw away. As copying gets easier, that kind of taste is what will set people apart."}),`
`,t(e.p,{children:"I'm comfortable borrowing here. My blog is where I dump things I've learned, and I've never put much thought into how it looks. Borrowing from designers who clearly did seemed better than leaving it plain. I plan to use opportunities like this to build up my own taste and apply it to the products and experiences I build."}),`
